From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Ryan Murray <81541646+RyanTheTide@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sun, 8 Nov 2026 23:48:01 +1100
Subject: [PATCH] media: apple: ave: add a speed-over-quality control

Add a driver-private boolean control, "Prioritize Speed Over Quality"
(V4L2_CID_APPLE_AVE_PRIORITIZE_SPEED), off by default. It is
VideoToolbox's PrioritizeEncodingSpeedOverQuality: macOS changes one
Start word from 3 to 5 for it, the word the driver called the size
class (3 above 720p). With 5 there, HEVC encodes 1.7-1.8 times faster
for about 12% more bits at the same PSNR; H.264 does not get faster
(on macOS either), so the control only applies to 8-bit HEVC above
1280x720 (Main 10 is not measured yet).

macOS 27.0 also changes a per-frame Process word from 3 to 5. Its
counterpart in this firmware's layout, the low-delay word at Process
+0xcf8, is not the same field: writing 5 there corrupts the coded
pictures (PSNR-Y 16 dB), so it is left alone. With only the Start word
the bitstreams match what macOS writes in speed mode to the byte
average.

The control is read when streaming starts. With the control off, every
command the driver builds is byte-identical to before.

Also expose the constant-quality table lookup for a KUnit test, and
document constant quality and the new control.

Signed-off-by: Ryan Murray <81541646+RyanTheTide@users.noreply.github.com>
Assisted-by: LLM
(cherry picked from commit 556fa11552bc2db5cfbba44686f3c837afe6d5a3)
